ate :llm with :output-schemas that extracts a typed provisions vector PER contract before the diff step — the diff :llm consumes the structured vectors, not raw text\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - contract version-vs-version diff (e.g. provider contract v2.0 vs v3.1 — produces provision-level differences + recommendation on which to prefer)\n  - vendor-contract negotiation review (compare current draft vs prior version)\n  - any two-document structural comparison where provision-level diff matters\n\n#### Alternative #3 — Risk-analysis (confidence: 0.85)\nWhy this fits: Risk-analysis is highly relevant for the 'impact analysis on current liability exposure' and 'executive summary' requirements. The use of a :risk-matrix (HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW) fits the need to address the 'Limitation of Liability' cap.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nRisk-analysis trees identify and categorize risks/obligations from a document into a per-item :risk-matrix (HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW + justification) plus an :executive-summary. The proven shape is per-section :map-each with structured :output-schemas: split by section delimiter, classify each section under bounded concurrency, aggregate the matrix, then synthesize the executive summary. Always use :output-schemas to constrain severity to an enum — free-form strings drift across iterations and break aggregation.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - identifies and categorizes risks/obligations in a document with severity ratings\n  - produces a risk-matrix (per-obligation HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW classification + justification) + executive-summary\n  - classification-flavored extraction; per-clause/per-section processing is the natural decomposition\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** per-section :map-each with structured output schema reliably classifies risks across long documents (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: document has natural sections (clauses, paragraphs) AND each section can be independently classified\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:chunk-document {:from :document :delimiter \"\\n\\n\" :into :sections}] [:map-each {:from :sections :as :section :into :results :max-concurrency 3} [:llm {:reads [:section] :writes [:risks] :output-schemas {:risks [:vector [:map [:obligation :string] [:severity [:enum :high :medium :low]] [:justification :string]]]}}]] [:aggregate {:from :results :writes [:risk-matrix]}] [:llm {:reads [:document :risk-matrix] :writes [:executive-summary]}] [:final {:keys [:risk-matrix :executive-summary]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** single :llm classification on a long document misses many obligations because of attention-budget exhaustion (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: document is >5KB AND tree is a single :llm without per-section chunking\n    - Recommended fix: split into sections (use :chunk-document with :delimiter) and run per-section :map-each :llm with bounded concurrency\n\n  - **Failure mode:** without :output-schemas, severity classifications drift to inconsistent strings ('high', 'HIGH', 'High', 'critical') across iterations — downstream aggregation breaks (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the per-section :llm writes severity as a free-form string\n    - Recommended fix: always declare :output-schemas with severity as [:enum :high :medium :low] so the model is constrained to consistent values\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - contract risk assessment (per-clause obligation extraction)\n  - regulatory-document risk inventory (per-section compliance review)\n  - any classification-heavy extraction where consistent enum values matter\n\n#### Alternative #4 — Risk-analysis (