orpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:spec] :writes [:artifact] :output-schemas {:artifact [:map [:file-path :string] [:language :string] [:imports [:vector :string]] [:code :string]]}}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** iterate code via draft → critique → refined-code when the code is non-trivial — single-pass code has higher defect rates than draft-critique-refine for the same prompt (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the code involves multiple interacting decisions (error handling / edge cases / type contracts)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:spec] :writes [:draft-code]}] [:llm {:reads [:draft-code :spec] :writes [:critique]}] [:llm {:reads [:draft-code :critique] :writes [:refined-code]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** code-building without an explicit :spec input (just a goal string) produces code that maps to the model's first guess at what the goal meant — divergence from intent surfaces only at test time (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the goal is ambiguous OR the consequences of wrong-interpretation are expensive\n    - Recommended fix: always pipe code-building's :reads through a design stage first; the design's :spec field is the input to code-building, not the raw goal\n\n  - **Failure mode:** single-shot generation of a long file (>200 lines) produces code with internal inconsistencies — function signatures mismatch their callers, imports declared but unused (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the artifact is a long file with multiple cohesive sections\n    - Recommended fix: scaffold via :code-built outline (top-of-file imports / function-declarations / glue), then :map-each per-section generation, then :code-stitched final — preserves consistency across section