idence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: quality has clear dimensions that can be enumerated upfront (accuracy, completeness, tone)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:writes [:score :issues :recommendation] :output-schemas {:score [:and :int [:>= 0] [:<= 10]] :issues [:vector :string] :recommendation [:enum :ship :revise :discard]}}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** using the SAME model for draft and critique correlates errors — the model that drafted a flawed argument may grade it as fine (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: draft and critique use the same model AND the quality dimensions involve subtle judgment\n    - Recommended fix: use different models for draft vs critique (e.g., draft with gemini, critique with claude) OR add a third independent reviewer model to break ties\n\n  - **Failure mode:** no retry means the loop ships a flawed draft with a low grade rather than fixing it — fine for auditing, wrong for production output (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the loop is in a path that emits to end-users (the artifact is the output, not the grade)\n    - Recommended fix: use iterative-refinement (draft → critique → revise) when the loop is in the output path, OR add retry-on-low-grade via producer-validator semantics\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - essay grading: draft answer → grade with rubric → surface to instructor\n  - code review automation: PR diff → critique against style/correctness rules → flag for human review\n  - blog post quality check: draft post → grade for accuracy/tone/completeness → recommend ship/revise/discard\n\n#### Alternative #1 — Draft + critique (confidence: 0.89)\nWhy this fits: This fingerprint shares the same content as the class above, emphasizing the structured critiq