termittent nil from shared state / timing)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:symptom :code] :writes [:candidate-causes]}] [:code {:reads [:candidate-causes] :writes [:repro] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:repro :deterministic-failing-case})}] [:llm {:reads [:repro :code] :writes [:root-cause :fix]}] [:final {:keys [:root-cause :fix]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** enumerate candidate causes upfront and rule them out with evidence before converging — bisect the search space rather than fixing the first plausible guess (which often masks the real cause) (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the symptom has multiple plausible causes (timing, shared state, environment, off-by-one)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:writes [:hypotheses]}] [:llm {:reads [:hypotheses :evidence] :writes [:ruled-out :remaining]}] [:llm {:reads [:remaining] :writes [:root-cause :minimal-fix]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** jumping straight to a code edit on the first hypothesis (a code-building instinct) without a deterministic repro produces a fix that may paper over the symptom while the real defect remains — and you cannot prove it is fixed (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the symptom is intermittent OR the root cause is not yet evidenced\n    - Recommended fix: reproduce deterministically first, name the root cause with evidence, then make the minimal change and re-run the repro\n\n  - **Failure mode:** an over-broad 'fix' that rewrites surrounding code while chasing a bug introduces new regressions and obscures which change actually resolved the defect (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the bug is localized and a targeted change suffices\n    - Recommended fix: scope the fix to the proven root cause; keep unrelated code untouched so the repro pinpoints the effective change\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - debug why the integration test checkout-flow-test fails intermittently with a nil cart-id and make it deterministic\n  - fix the off-by-one bug in the pagination loop so the final page renders its last row instead of dropping it\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                                     :confidence 0.7\n                                     :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :weaknesses [{:trait \"<what fails AND why>\"\n                                      :avoid-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                      :recommended-alternative \"<concrete fix>\"\n                                      :confidence 0.7\n                                      :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :representative-uses [\"<concrete task this child shipped on>\"]\n                        :avoid-when