ing]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** extract specific QUOTES alongside summaries in the per-chunk step — the synthesis stage can then ground claims to source quotes, supporting traceability (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the briefing audience may verify claims against source material AND traceability matters\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:chunk] :writes [:key-points :supporting-quotes] :output-schemas {:key-points [:vector :string] :supporting-quotes [:vector :string]}}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** the synthesis stage's context can overflow when per-chunk extracts are too rich AND the chunk count is high — synthesizer truncates or hallucinates connections (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: per-chunk extraction emits >500 tokens per chunk AND chunk count is >15\n    - Recommended fix: either reduce per-chunk extraction granularity (key-points-only, no quotes) OR add an intermediate :code stage that selects top-K per-chunk extracts before synthesis\n\n  - **Failure mode:** without a grounding judge, the synthesis stage can fabricate connections between per-chunk extracts that aren't actually in the source — a 'plausible narrative' that doesn't survive verification (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the briefing is going to a decision-maker AND no grounding check is wired downstream\n    - Recommended fix: wire a grounding judge on the briefing output that checks each claim against the source quotes — catches fabricated connections before the briefing ships\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - executive briefing from a long research report: chunked extraction → final cohesive 3-paragraph briefing\n  - situation-report generation from a stream of incident logs: per-incident extraction 